  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nSurface high pressure was centered across western PA early this\nmorning. The high will drift slowly eastward today and tonight,\nmaintaining dry weather across the Upper Ohio Valley region.\nRising 500 mb heights should result in temperatures a few\ndegrees warmer than Monday, with highs just above seasonable \nlevels.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nA broad upper trough is expected to approach the region from the\nnorthern Plains and Upper Midwest region Wednesday, as a closed\nlow across the Tennessee Valley drifts north. The low is progged\nto phase with the approaching trough, as moisture and lift begin\nto increase across the Upper Ohio Valley region. Scattered\nshowers and thunderstorms are expected by Wednesday afternoon\ninto the evening. The overall flow is generally weak, with\nminimal shear. ML CAPE is generally progged from 500-1000 j/kg.\nIt appears any severe weather potential is limited. \n\nThe broad upper trough is then progged to persist across the\nGreat Lakes and Upper Ohio Valley region through the weekend,\ninto early next week. Individual shortwaves are expected to\ntrack through the main trough, resulting in daily shower and\nthunderstorm chances, with diurnal coverage trends. While\nuncertainty still exists, it appears the strongest wave is\nprogged for a Friday into Saturday passage. While there will be\na potential for severe storms and locally heavy rainfall, it\nremains too early for specific details, such as how cloud cover\naffects instability, etc, and how this affects the severe\npotential. This will be monitored over the next few days.",
